  "transcript": "# Scrutiny of the draft Heathrow Expansion National Policy Statement\n\n## What the climate test must cover\n> The test in the draft HENPS must be explicit: it should judge Heathrow expansion against carbon budgets, the 2050 net zero target, and the airport’s full eventual emissions.\n\nChair: To start, please introduce yourselves and tell us how you would define “material impact” in the draft climate test.\nProfessor Forster: I'm a Climate Change Committee member and one of its aviation champions; my work is on aviation's climate effects.\nDr Richardson: I'm the Committee's chief analyst. On 'material impact', courts may ultimately interpret the phrase, but our view is clear.\nDr Richardson: You must judge Heathrow’s effect on the UK’s carbon budgets and on the 2050 net zero target, and include the airport when it's fully expanded.\nDr Richardson: You can't avoid responsibility just because the peak emissions happen after 2050; any additional emissions need an equivalent reduction elsewhere.\nAlex Mayer: Should 'material impact' be a quantitative threshold or a qualitative judgement?\nDr Richardson: It will partly be a judgement in the round. A single fixed number risks gaming the models; better to require a clear explanation of net zero compliance.\n\n\n## Does the draft test work as written?\n> As drafted it fails to require alignment with 2050 net zero and the airport’s full emissions—without fixing that, the test is ineffective.\n\nAlex Mayer: Your advice doesn't give a specific emissions ceiling that would make expansion unviable. Should there be a high-end threshold?\nDr Richardson: Since net zero is an absolute, any additional emissions must be countered—through SAF, removals or both—so the focus should be on how to get back to zero.\nAlex Mayer: Some witnesses say the current test can't fail anyone. Do you agree?\nDr Richardson: If you adopt our changes—include 2050, include full expansion—then the test could stop a project. As it stands, it effectively isn't a real test.\nAlex Mayer: If those changes aren't made, should the whole NPS be rejected?\nDr Richardson: A test that omits the net zero target is no test. Government policy needs to make the path to net zero explicit before a DCO is granted.\n\n\n## Offsets, SAF and who pays\n> Aviation can't rely on other sectors to compensate; the industry must bear the cost of cuts and removals, but current SAF and removals are not yet in place.\n\nDr Arthur: You say we can't rely on carbon savings elsewhere in the economy to offset Heathrow's additional emissions. Why not?\nProfessor Forster: Other sectors will be mostly decarbonised by 2050, and many remaining emissions are methane from agriculture—not a substitute for CO2 reductions.\nDr Arthur: So are you saying the trade is only between direct aviation cuts (like SAF) and engineered removals?\nProfessor Forster: Yes. Either aviation reduces its own CO2, or society scales up removals. Our advice is that aviation should pay for those removals.\nDr Arthur: Can we be confident SAF and removals will be available and affordable by the time needed?\nProfessor Forster: Not yet. Current production of the next-generation SAF and of removals is essentially zero. Government policy and investment must start now to scale them up.\nJacob Collier: How much extra removals would Heathrow expansion require?\nProfessor Forster: About 4.5 million tonnes a year extra for Heathrow's expansion; our net zero pathway assumes roughly 36 million tonnes of removals in total.\nChris Hinchliff: You recommend the industry must abate 100% of its emissions before a DCO. What mechanism and timetable do you expect?\nDr Richardson: Key moment is the revised jet zero strategy due next year. Mechanisms could include emissions trading, a stronger SAF mandate, or a removals mandate—what matters is add-up to 100% by 2050.\nChris Hinchliff: If aviation pays, won't flight costs rise so much that demand collapses and the expansion is pointless?\nDr Richardson: Pricing carbon will raise costs and reduce demand growth. Our modelling shows demand growth would be lower than the Government's forecasts—21% vs 64%—and a return flight could add about £150 by 2050, phased in.\nProfessor Forster: Putting the cost on flyers is fairer: most people don't fly, and aviation should bear the cost of the pollution it creates.\n\n\n## Who carries the risk and what extra safeguards are needed\n> Developers should bear the commercial risk of expansion; Government must set a credible Jet Zero pathway, include contingency measures, and require ongoing checking if consent is granted.\n\nChris Hinchliff: If Heathrow expands and aviation overshoots the trajectory, what contingency measures should exist?\nDr Richardson: Government needs a clear Jet Zero plan and policies that lock in the abatement route. Contingencies could be stronger regulation, mandates for removals, or tighter SAF obligations.\nDaniel Francis: Should Heathrow face ongoing climate performance checks if it gets consent?\nProfessor Forster: Yes. The Jet Zero strategy should set the government-wide pathway; delivery must be checked regularly and the DCO decision tied to that credible pathway.\nDr Richardson: The risk of demand not materialising should sit with developers, not taxpayers. Developers need to know the policy pathway before investing.\n\n\n## Non-CO2 effects and urgency\n> Non-CO2 warming (like contrails) can and should be included quantitatively; the Committee sees aviation as off-track and calls for stronger, sooner action.\n\nChair: We disagree with DFT about whether non-CO2 effects can be quantified. Can they?\nProfessor Forster: Yes. There's uncertainty, but enough evidence exists to quantify these effects rather than ignore them. They can change the overall climate impact estimate.\nChair: Do you have a practical approach?\nProfessor Forster: In our Seventh Carbon Budget work we proposed that non-CO2 warming should contribute no additional warming after 2050—one way to set a clear limit.\nChair: Should the decision be paused until Government and the Committee agree a method?\nProfessor Forster: More work is needed. But if Heathrow were approved it could run trials to reduce non-CO2 effects, for example on the north Atlantic corridor where contrails are important.\nChris Hinchliff: Given recent climate signs and the WMO's updated timings, are we in an emergency and should stronger measures apply now?\nProfessor Forster: Aviation is not on track for the Sixth Carbon Budget. We need immediate additional policies and prepared contingencies; industry must start paying for carbon now.\nChair: Finally, is the climate test principally for the project promoter or for government policy?\nDr Richardson: Both, but mainly it's about government policy. The Government must set the aviation-wide pathway; promoters can then address the parts they control, like airside emissions and supporting SAF.\n",
